Please evaluate the following table-sharding design as a senior backend / database architect for a production distributed payment and wallet system.
Context:
I am going to implement table sharding for the main tables in two services. (only table sharding, not db sharding)

ledger-service
    Account
    LedgerTxn
    LedgerEntry
    Outbox
wallet-service:
    WalletAction
    WalletReservation
    WalletTransaction
    BalanceSnapshot
    Wallet
    WalletAccountMapping

pls evaluate these shard designs:
Account, LedgerTxn, Wallet, WalletTransaction:
    A: shard by business create time (day), it is better for time range query, but today's table/current page becomes too hot.
    B: shard by internal-id, insertion and query becomes average, no hot spot. but time range query need to go through every tables.
I prefer B, since hot spot could affect performance.

LedgerEntry:
    A: business create time (which is same as LedgerTxn business create time, they are created together and read-only).
        it is better for time range query, but today's table becomes too hot.
    B: account.internal-id, one ledger entry belongs to one account, so that query by account only touches one table.
    C: ledger-txn.internal-id, ledger-txn query entries only touches one table.
ledger-entry is usually used for accountancy, reconciliation and report. we could need to do these operations per ledger-txn, per account, and for a time window.
I prefer plan B, since query is usually for single/a few account.
ledger-txn usually only have a few (not too many) entries, so they usually land into a few tables.
for large query, I can tolerate with querying all tables for now, and I suggest to use a ES or other middleware for large query.

WalletAction:
    A: business create time, same reason.
    B: wallet.internal-id, one wallet action belongs to one wallet, so that query by wallet only touches one table.
    C: wallet-txn.internal-id. wallet-txn query action only touches one table.
I prefer plan B, since:
    hot spot really affect performance.
    user usually have a few wallets (not too many) and they could query for a time window. (so wallet land in a few tables, not all of them)
    a wallet-txn usually does not have massive actions. (so actions land in a few tables, not all of them)
    querying single/a few wallets could be a frequent operation.
    although system query like reconciliation could touch all tables, but that is rare and predictable.

WalletAccountMapping:
    wallet.internal-id, only query by wallet.internal-id

Outbox:
    shard by the outbox.event-id, which is generally the internal-id of that entity. usually query by event-id

BalanceSnapshot: wallet.internal-id
    snapshot is usually queried by wallet

WalletReservation:
    A: balance-snapshot.internal-id, query by snapshot only touches one table.
    B: wallet-transaction.internal-id, query by transaction only touches one table.
    C: business time, same reason.
I prefer plan A, basically same reason as WalletAction, one wallet-transaction usually only have a few reservations.

Rules:
- my system uses innoDB, and does not allow join operation in db level.
- internal-id has not implemented now, but you can refer the design in ai/codex/feature/id-design/id-design-implementation.md
- sql is in standard/sql
- inspect code when necessary
- do not change code
- only table sharding, not db sharding
- when I say shard by internal-id, I mean shard by hash(internal-id)

